From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: server: a pipe end reports how much it can write (WriteQuotaAvailable)

FilePipeLocalInformation.WriteQuotaAvailable was always 0 (a FIXME; Wine's
own ntdll pipe test marks the right values todo_wine). The Cygwin/MSYS
runtime reads it before writing to a pty and waits, polling every
millisecond, until it covers the write: with 0 it waited forever, so no
program in Git for Windows' terminal (mintty) ever printed anything -- bash
ran, spinning in fhandler_pty_slave::write, and the window stayed empty.

It is now what the other end can buffer less what is queued there unread --
the reader's buffer_size, which is what a write passes without blocking --
and, before a connection, the end's own configured quota. An empty pipe
created with 200 outbound and 100 inbound reports 200 at the server end and
100 at the client, as Windows and Wine's test expect.

Gate: test/pipequota-gate.sh -- empty, after writes, after reads.
---
--- a/server/named_pipe.c
+++ b/server/named_pipe.c
@@ -854,7 +854,20 @@
             pipe_info->ReadDataAvailable   = pipe_end_get_avail( pipe_end );
 
             pipe_info->OutboundQuota       = pipe->outsize;
-            pipe_info->WriteQuotaAvailable = 0; /* FIXME */
+            /* What this end can write before a write would block: the
+             * other end's buffer less what is queued there unread (the
+             * reader's buffer_size is what pipe_end_write lets through at
+             * once). Programs poll this before writing -- the Cygwin/MSYS
+             * runtime waits for it to cover a write, and with 0 no program in
+             * Git Bash's terminal could ever write. */
+            if (pipe_end->connection)
+            {
+                data_size_t queued = pipe_end_get_avail( pipe_end->connection );
+                data_size_t quota = pipe_end->connection->buffer_size;
+                pipe_info->WriteQuotaAvailable = quota > queued ? quota - queued : 0;
+            }
+            else pipe_info->WriteQuotaAvailable = pipe_end->obj.ops == &pipe_server_ops
+                ? pipe->outsize : pipe->insize;
             pipe_info->NamedPipeState      = pipe_end->state;
             pipe_info->NamedPipeEnd        = pipe_end->obj.ops == &pipe_server_ops
                 ? FILE_PIPE_SERVER_END : FILE_PIPE_CLIENT_END;
